          %p
            The classification matches that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+invariant+one"} Hopf invariant one
            maps. See there for more.
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cross+product"} cross product
          %h2#generalized_hurwitz_theorem Generalized Hurwitz Theorem
          %p
            As describe in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Elduque21"} Elduque (2021)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition+algebras"} composition algebras
            that are not necessarily unital, but that are
            %em para-unital
            or even
            %em non-unital
            (see
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/composition+algebra#definition"} here
            for the relevant definitions).
          %p The Generalized Hurwitz Theorem then becomes the statement that the only Euclidean composition algebras over the real numbers are, up to isomorphism:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Seven unital algebras: real numbers, complex numbers, quaternions, and octonions (along with their
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/composition+algebra#split_composition_algebras"} split
                companions).
            %li
              %p Seven para-unital algebras, corresponding to the para- counterparts of the unital algebras above.
            %li
              %p
                Two eight-dimensional non-unital algebras: the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Okubo algebra
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Okubo+algebra"} ?
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒪
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
                and its split counterpart
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mi s
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_s
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adolf+Hurwitz"} Adolf Hurwitz
            (1859–1919), published posthumously in 1923.
          %ul
